<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 10 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">Before the State</h1><p class="path-tagline">Cultures known from their remains, from the first settled hunters to Iron Age Europe’s walled towns.</p><p class="path-intro">Long before writing and states, people lived in ways known only from what they left in the ground. The cultures on this path are archaeologists’ names for such remains: pottery, stone points, graves, houses and metalwork. They show hunter-gatherers making pottery in Japan around 14,500 BCE and living in permanent villages in the Levant, farmers building settlements of up to three thousand structures, herders crossing the steppe in wagons, and Iron Age communities growing rich on salt and trade. The path runs from the Jōmon to the La Tène culture, whose walled towns became Roman cities.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Timekeeper</a> · <a href="/start">the Builder</a></p><p class="path-print-row"><button type="button" class="ws-print path-print">Print this path</button></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/culture/jomon-culture"><span class="path-step-ph">1</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · c. 14000 BCE – c. 300 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Jōmon culture</span><span class="path-step-why">For some 14,000 years the people of the Japanese archipelago lived mainly by hunting, gathering and fishing, and they made the world’s oldest known pottery around 14,500 BCE.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/culture/natufians"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-carmel.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Profile · c. 13000 BCE – c. 9500 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Natufians</span><span class="path-step-why">Hunter-gatherers of the Levant who lived in settled villages, some with round stone houses and the first known food stores, before farming began.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/culture/clovis-culture"><span class="path-step-ph">3</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Profile · c. 11100 BCE – c. 10800 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Clovis culture</span><span class="path-step-why">The earliest widely recognised culture of North America, mobile hunters of mammoth and bison with fluted stone points, though not the first people of the Americas.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/culture/mehrgarh-culture"><span class="path-step-ph">4</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Profile · c. 7000 BCE – c. 2500 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Mehrgarh culture</span><span class="path-step-why">Near the Bolan Pass, one of the earliest known places in South Asia where people farmed barley and wheat and herded sheep, goats and cattle.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/culture/ubaid-culture"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ubaid-period.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Profile · c. 6500 BCE – c. 3800 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Ubaid culture</span><span class="path-step-why">It left the earliest known settlements on the southern Mesopotamian plain and the first temples there, but still no real cities: those came in the Uruk period.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Ubaid period is a prehistoric period of Mesopotamia, from about 6500 to 3800 BCE, named after Tell al-Ubaid near Ur.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/culture/cucuteni-trypillia-culture"><span class="path-step-ph">6</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Profile · c. 5050 BCE – c. 2950 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Cucuteni–Trypillia culture</span><span class="path-step-why">Farmers between the Carpathians and the Dnieper who built some of the largest settlements in Eurasia, and burned and rebuilt them every 60 to 80 years.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/culture/yamnaya-culture"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kurgan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Profile · c. 3300 BCE – c. 2600 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Yamnaya culture</span><span class="path-step-why">Herders of the Pontic–Caspian steppe who moved in carts and wagons, buried their dead under mounds and are widely held to have spoken a stage of Proto-Indo-European.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/culture/bell-beaker-culture"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/knowth.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Profile · c. 2800 BCE – c. 1800 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Bell Beaker culture</span><span class="path-step-why">Named after a drinking vessel shaped like an upside-down bell, it spread copper, gold and bronze working along sea and river routes from Portugal to Ireland and Hungary.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/culture/hallstatt-culture"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hochdorf-chieftains-grave.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Profile · c. 1200 BCE – c. 450 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Hallstatt culture</span><span class="path-step-why">The salt mines of Hallstatt made its community unusually rich, and late in the period princely seats such as the Heuneburg traded with the Greek and Etruscan world.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Hallstatt culture was the main archaeological culture of western and central Europe in the early Iron Age, from about 800 to 450 BCE, and is commonly associated with speakers of Proto-Celtic.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/culture/la-tene-culture"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battersea-shield.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">10 · Profile · c. 450 BCE – c. 50 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">La Tène culture</span><span class="path-step-why">The successor of Hallstatt from about 450 BCE, known for its curving metalwork and its walled oppida, many of which, such as Lutetia, became Roman cities.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The La Tène culture was the late Iron Age culture of Celtic Europe, from about 450 BCE until the Roman conquest.</span></span></a></li></ol>
